``"nearest"`` (default): the value of the cell holding the point;
``"bilinear"``: interpolated from the four nearest cell centres
(weights renormalised over those with data).

``"sites"``: 365 records of 31 species (columns ``num``, ``genus``,
``species``, ``x``, ``y``; projected metres, Australia);
``"tree"``: a Nexus phylogeny of those species (``Genus:sp1``...).

``resistances``: ids and the voltage over the strength at each point
(one-to-all; -1 where nothing was solved) or 0 (all-to-one);
``cum_current``, ``max_current``, and the per-point maps in ``maps``.

``"maungawhau"``: elevation of the Maungawhau volcano (Auckland), 87 x 61
cells of 10 m (New Zealand Map Grid), the classic hiking example;
``"europe"``: land (1) and sea (missing) of Europe, 135 x 195 cells of
1/3 degree (longitude/latitude);
``"gen_dist"``: genetic distances between 23 European populations of the
Y-chromosome haplogroup R1b1b2 (Balaresque et al. 2010, PLoS Biology
8: e1000285), a CSV table with a ``population`` column;
``"pop_coord"``: the longitude (``x``) and latitude (``y``) of those
populations.

On longitude/latitude grids: ``"c"`` for least-cost distances;
``"r"`` for random walks, which also scales north-south connections
(conductances) by the cosine of their mean latitude.

A row per two points: ``from``, ``to`` (1..), ``network`` (map units;
