doc.x:
The habitat objects (8-connected foreground components) of areas A_i give:

- ECA (equivalent connected area) = sqrt(sum of A_i²), the size of a single
  object with the same connectivity;
- DOC (degree of coherence) = ECA / total habitat area, in %;
- restoration potential = 100 - DOC;
- RAC (relative area of habitat) = habitat area / valid area, in %;
- APS (average patch size) and CNOA (critical new object area).

i:
``"drop"``: return the records that pass every test; ``"flag"``:
return all of them with a ``flag_<test>`` column per test run (True =
suspicious) and ``flagged`` (any).

``"bradypus"``: 116 occurrence records of the brown-throated sloth
(*Bradypus variegatus*), a CSV table with ``species``, ``lon``, ``lat``;
``"acaule"``: 1366 GBIF records of the wild potato *Solanum acaule*
(with country, locality, coordinate uncertainty, institution and basis
of record; 284 without coordinates), the classic data for cleaning;
``"bioclim"``: nine WorldClim bioclimatic variables (bio1, bio5, bio6,
bio7, bio8, bio9, bio12, bio16, bio17; temperatures in tenths of a
degree), 192 x 186 cells of 0.5 degree over Central and South America;
``"biome"``: the WWF biomes (1-14) on the same grid.

doc.x:
The steps, in order, each one a function of its own that can be called
alone (and each argument here takes ``False`` to skip the step, a number
for its main setting, a dict of that function's arguments, or a
function of yours):

1. ``clean`` – `clean_occurrences()` (drop records at centroids,
   capitals, institutions, in the sea, outliers...);
2. ``one_per_cell`` – `one_per_cell()`;
3. ``thin`` – `thin()` (a distance in metres; off by default);
4. ``area`` – the accessible area: `accessible_area()` (a buffer
   distance in metres, its arguments, or your polygons; default the
   whole extent of ``env``);
5. ``predictors`` – `select_predictors()` in the area (a VIF
   threshold, default 10);
6. ``background`` – `background()` points in the area (a number,
   default 10 000);
7. `dataset()` – predictor values at presences and background;
8. ``folds`` – cross-validation folds: a number k of spatial blocks
   (`spatial_blocks()`, default 5) or a dict with ``method`` one of
   ``"blocks"``, ``"clusters"``, ``"quadrants"``, ``"checkerboard"``,
   ``"random"``, ``"jackknife"`` and the method's arguments; blocks take
   ``size="auto"`` for the predictors' range of autocorrelation
   (`autocorrelation_range()`).

Every random step uses ``seed``, so the same call gives the same data.
``data.report`` counts what each step removed, ``data.settings`` holds
every choice (for the methods of a paper) and ``data.redo(...)`` repeats
the preparation with some settings changed.

i:
``"countryref"``: centroids of the countries and provinces and the
country capitals (5305 rows: ``iso3``, ``iso2``, ``name``, ``type``,
``centroid.lon`` / ``.lat``, ``capital.lon`` / ``.lat``...);
``"institutions"``: 11 601 biodiversity institutions (herbaria,
museums, zoos, botanical gardens, universities) with coordinates;
``"aohi"``: artificial hotspot occurrence inventory (Park et al. 2023);
``"buffland"`` / ``"buffsea"``: the land / the sea of Natural Earth
(1:50 million) widened by one degree; ``"land110"``, ``"land50"``,
``"land10"``: Natural Earth land at 1:110, 1:50 and 1:10 million
(4.1.0 / 4.1.0 / 5.1.1); ``"urban50"``: urban areas (4.1.0);
``"countries50"``: countries (5.1.1, lowercase field names).

